---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CopyBlock from '../components/CopyBlock.astro';
const GH = 'https://github.com/nightswatchhq/nuthatch';
---
<Layout
  path="/install"
  title="Install Nuthatch"
  description="Install Nuthatch v4.3.1: a one-line installer or prebuilt binaries for macOS (Apple Silicon) and Linux x86_64, container images, or build from source with cargo. Provenance-attested release artifacts, nine built-in chains, portable nests and fault quarantine."
>
  <section class="page">
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <p class="section-label">Install</p>
      <h1>Get Nuthatch running.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Embedded mode is a single binary with no external services. Pick whichever
        route you trust. Every one lands you at the same place: <code>nuthatch init</code>,
        then <code>nuthatch dev</code>.
      </p>

      <p class="prerelease-note">
          <strong>v4.3.1 is out</strong>: it fixes a wrong answer in 4.3.0, where a join under a memory budget could drop matches. Take it if you run 4.3.0. 4.3.0 before it lowered the SQL engine's peak memory under a budget, 4.2.1 rebuilt the admin page, 4.2.0 fixed a memory regression in 4.1.1, and 4.1.1 closed the findings of a cold audit of 4.1.0, among them a <code>/sql</code> statement that could stop the process. <strong>4.1.0 changed the SQL engine</strong>: analytical SQL runs on Burrmill, our engine on DataFusion, and DuckDB is no longer in the binary. Nothing is re-indexed, but a statement that needs more memory than its session's budget is refused rather than spilled, so a join-heavy nest may need <code>analytics.memory_limit</code> raised; <code>emit dune</code> is gone; and the Linux binary needs glibc 2.35. Each is a binary swap and a restart. 4.0.2 before them: an <code>eth_getLogs</code> result cap that arrives under the rate-limit code <code>-32005</code>, as <code>rpc.mevblocker.io</code> sends it, is narrowed to the range the endpoint suggests instead of being retried at the same width. Take it if you created a mainnet nest on 4.0.1. In 4.0.1 before it, a new Ethereum mainnet nest is given two public endpoints that serve history, <code>mainnet.gateway.tenderly.co</code> and <code>rpc.mevblocker.io</code>, in place of two that could not serve a backfill. An existing nest keeps the endpoints it was given; pass <code>--rpc</code> to move it. 4.0.0 before it is the stable line. It is 3.13.3 plus three small fixes, and the major version is a promise about later releases rather than a claim about what changed: from here to the last 4.x, config keeps working with the same meaning, a data directory upgrades in place with no re-index or migration command, and no HTTP route, response field, generated table or column, or MCP tool is removed, renamed or retyped (<a href="/docs/operate/upgrades/#the-4x-promise">the 4.x promise</a>). The fixes: a backfill whose RPC endpoints have pruned its history says so and names the remedy, an archive-capable RPC via <code>--rpc</code> or <code>rpc_urls</code>; <code>nuthatch sql</code> and the MCP SQL tool print columns in the query's order, and a <code>/sql</code> response gains a <code>columns</code> array naming them; and the first-run demo starts near the tip with <code>nuthatch dev --backfill 300</code>. From 3.13.3 it is a binary swap, with no storage migration, config change, NID change or re-index. 3.13.3 before it made <code>nuthatch check</code> agree with <code>dev</code> about incremental entities, made every entity <code>port-emit</code> writes startable, had <code>nest bundle</code> refuse to publish credentials, stopped log lines keeping an RPC URL's password, and made a restart, an entity fault and a finality violation all report truthfully on a quiet chain. Anyone who authors entities or ports a subgraph should take it. 3.13.2 before it was a hardening pass: Arbitrum timestamps are right through <code>arb1.arbitrum.io</code> (fetched as headers, so a backfill through that keyless endpoint runs at a few times chain speed; a keyed RPC avoids it), the analytics spill cap is enforced and off a tmpfs <code>/tmp</code>, authored entity columns reach SQL typed, and a misspelt <code>nuthatch.toml</code> key fails <code>nuthatch check</code> with a suggestion. 3.13.1 before it fixed what an end-to-end run of 3.13.0's runtime admin API found against a real chain: names and a restart mid-move that left a runtime unable to start, a move that broke a dataset two tenants shared, and identical mounts reported as failed. Anyone hosting nests should take it. Since 3.13.0 a runtime runs its own nests: hand it a NID over the admin API and
          it fetches the nest from a registry, verifies and indexes it, and can dry-run, suspend, move and
          reclaim it (<a href="/docs/operate/hosting-nests">Host nests for others</a>). 3.12.2 before it moved
          Wasmtime past two security advisories and let a runtime start with nothing mounted. Of the five
          releases before those, 3.9.0 adds
          <code>nest nid</code>, which prints the identity a nest's data is stored under, and <code>offchain pull</code>, a one-shot fetch that seals a JSON price feed as
          a snapshot authored entities can read. 3.11.0 takes block timestamps from the logs instead of a
          header per block, stops idle polls paying for a reorg check, sets the default poll interval to
          the chain's block time (12 s on mainnet, where it was 2 s everywhere), and adds
          <code>--rpc-fallback</code> for an endpoint asked only while the others fail. 3.12.0 changes
          nothing in the published binaries. 3.12.1 runs each <code>/sql</code> statement once rather than
          twice, and answers a query cut off by the time budget with 504 rather than 400. For most nests it
          is a binary swap and a restart; a script that mounts over the admin API adds <code>?wait=true</code>,
          because from 3.13.0 a mount answers 202 while it runs. Three things to read first: from 3.10.0 the GraphQL routes exist
          only in a build with <code>--features graph</code>, so the published binaries answer them with
          404; since 3.9.0 an entity's SQL must live in <code>entities/&lt;name&gt;.sql</code>; and an
          <code>eth_call</code> error that is not a revert now stalls the nest rather than being stored as a
          null, so a nest calling through a pruned node wants an archive endpoint.
        </p>

      <h2 class="ih">Shell script</h2>
      <p>Detects your platform, downloads the matching binary from the latest GitHub release, verifies its SHA-256 checksum, and installs it to <code>~/.local/bin</code> (override with <code>NUTHATCH_INSTALL_DIR</code>). Covers macOS Apple Silicon and Linux x86_64; other targets fall back to <code>cargo</code>. No Intel Mac binary is published: on an Intel Mac, build from source with Rust 1.95.0 (below). The Linux binary is dynamically linked and needs glibc 2.35 or newer and no C++ runtime: Debian 12 and Ubuntu 22.04 qualify. RHEL 9 and Amazon Linux 2023 ship glibc 2.34 and ran 4.0.x; from 4.1.0 they need the source build.</p>
      <CopyBlock command="curl -fsSL https://nuthatch-indexer.com/install.sh | sh" size="lg" />
      <p class="note">
        Piping to a shell asks for trust. Read it first:
        <a href="https://github.com/nightswatchhq/nuthatch-frontend/blob/main/public/install.sh" rel="noopener">the script is short and on GitHub</a>,
        or download it, read it, then run it.
      </p>

      <h2 class="ih">Cargo</h2>
      <p>Build from source straight from the repo, with Rust 1.95.0. This is the only route on a platform without a published binary, Intel Macs included (the Intel build has not been verified on Intel hardware). The toolchain pin is required, not advisory: <code>rust-toolchain.toml</code> does not apply to <code>cargo install --git</code>, and rustc 1.97 hits a <code>dbsp</code> ICE that installs nothing. No prebuilt binary in the trust path.</p>
      <CopyBlock command="rustup toolchain install 1.95.0" />
      <CopyBlock command="cargo +1.95.0 install --git https://github.com/nightswatchhq/nuthatch nuthatch" />
      <p class="note">
        Or grab a tarball and its <code>.sha256</code> yourself from the
        <a href="https://github.com/nightswatchhq/nuthatch/releases/latest" rel="noopener">latest GitHub release</a>:
        <code>nuthatch-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz</code>, <code>nuthatch-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz</code>,
        or <code>nuthatch-scaled-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz</code> for scaled mode.
      </p>

      <h2 class="ih">Homebrew <span class="tag">planned</span></h2>
      <p>macOS and Linuxbrew, once a tap is published.</p>
      <CopyBlock command="brew install nuthatch" />

      <h2 class="ih">Docker</h2>
      <p>
        Embedded mode needs no Docker at all - that is rather the point. Images are published for
        anyone who wants one, and for <a href="/docs/operate/scaled/">scaled mode</a>, where Postgres
        replaces the embedded hot store across a fleet.
      </p>
      <CopyBlock command='docker run --rm -v "$PWD/nest:/nest" -p 127.0.0.1:8288:8288 ghcr.io/nightswatchhq/nuthatch:4.3.1' prompt={true} />
      <p class="note">
        The image runs <code>dev</code> on the nest mounted at <code>/nest</code>, bound to
        <code>0.0.0.0:8288</code> inside the container, so publish it to loopback as above.
        <code>:4.3.1</code> and <code>:latest</code> are the embedded build; pin the version for anything
        you care about. <code>:4.3.1-scaled</code> carries the Postgres driver and the <code>worker</code>,
        <code>control</code> and <code>serve --hot-store</code> commands. <code>linux/amd64</code> only.
        The compose files live in the
        <a href="https://github.com/nightswatchhq/nuthatch" rel="noopener">repo</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="ih">First run</h2>
      <p>Point it at a contract and go. Public RPC defaults; no key required. Those endpoints are for trying it out, not for keeping it running: for that, point <code>--rpc</code> at your own node or a provider, which may charge for it.</p>
      <CopyBlock command="nuthatch init 0xA0b86991c6218b36c1d19D4a2e9Eb0cE3606eB48 --chain mainnet" />
      <CopyBlock command="nuthatch dev --backfill 300" />
      <p class="note">
        <code>init</code> resolves each contract's ABI (proxies included), scaffolds a nest, and
        writes a <code>schema.json</code>, an <code>llms.txt</code>, and a Claude Code skill. Pass
        several addresses to index a multi-contract nest. Omit <code>--chain</code> and <code>init</code>
        probes all nine built-in chains for the contract's bytecode. <code>--backfill 300</code> starts
        300 blocks behind the tip, so there are rows within seconds; without it <code>dev</code>
        backfills from the contract's deployment block, which for a contract with a long history wants
        your own archive-capable RPC. <code>dev</code> follows the tip and
        serves <code>/tables</code>, <code>/table/{'{'}name{'}'}</code>, read-only <code>/sql</code>,
        balances and <code>/metrics</code> on one local endpoint, which <code>nuthatch mcp</code> bridges
        to an agent.
      </p>

      <hr class="divider ih-div" />

      <h2 class="ih">Verify what you downloaded</h2>
      <p>
        Every release binary ships with a SHA-256 checksum file. The shell script checks the sum for
        you; do it yourself for the manual download.
      </p>
      <CopyBlock command="sha256sum -c nuthatch-<target>.tar.gz.sha256" prompt={true} />
      <div class="keybox">
        <span class="keybox-label">Build provenance</span>
        <p class="keybox-note">
          Every release artifact since 3.0 also carries a GitHub build-provenance attestation. A checksum says
          the file arrived intact; this verifies which repository and workflow built it. The repository
          constraint is load-bearing:
        </p>
        <CopyBlock command="gh attestation verify nuthatch-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz --repo nightswatchhq/nuthatch" />
      </div>

      <p class="backlink"><a href={GH} rel="noopener">Source, docs, and issues on GitHub →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4.5rem) clamp(4rem, 8vw, 6rem); }
  .page h1 { margin-bottom: 1.2rem; }
  .lede { color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 1.1rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .ih { margin-top: 2.8rem; margin-bottom: 0.8rem; font-size: 1.35rem; }
  .ih-div { margin-block: 2.8rem 0; }
  .page p { color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .page :global(.copyblock) { margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
  .note { font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--text-faint); }
  .prerelease-note {
    background: var(--pale-rust-bg); border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--rust); border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem; font-size: 0.92rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .ih .tag { font-size: 0.62rem; vertical-align: middle; margin-left: 0.4rem; }
  .keybox { margin-top: 1rem; background: var(--bg-elev); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 1.2rem 1.3rem; }
  .keybox-label { font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--text-faint); }
  .keybox-id { display: block; margin-top: 0.5rem; font-size: 0.82rem; color: var(--accent-ink); word-break: break-all; }
  .keybox-note { margin: 0.6rem 0 0; font-size: 0.82rem; }
  .backlink { margin-top: 2.5rem; }
  .backlink a { font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace; font-size: 0.9rem; }
</style>
